A||||8||dennis_6||3.4 Conversion (yes I searched the archives)||||1||
Z||000000||dennis_6||08-29-2001||01:46 PM||||I am just wanting to make sure of a few things.  I got a 3.4 kit last week that includes a flexplate (or atleast I think that is what it is), two mounts, starter template and an oil sender adapter.  If I go 3.4 TDC I will have to use the 3.4 air intake and fuel injection setup, wiring harness, computer, and have to cut a decklid spring.  If I go 3.4 pushrod I use everything from the heads up off my 2.8, keep my stock computer, and use the 3.4 injectors.  I just want to know if I am understanding right before I commit to one or the other.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Steve Normington||08-29-2001||03:39 PM||||For the 3.4 pushrod, you are correct with two notes.<BR>1) Make sure the engine is a 3.4 (iron heads) and not a 3400 (aluminum heads). The camarobird engines are 3.4's.<BR>2) If the engine is from a RWD, you will need to drill and tap two holes for the starter.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Alex4mula||08-29-2001||03:51 PM||||You can use your 2.8 injectors also and save some money. I have them in my 3.4 with good results.||||reg||1
Z||000003||pontiacs4me||08-29-2001||05:54 PM||||Sort of on the same topic, will 3.1 liter pushrod alum heads work on a 3.4?  And what FWD cars had a 3.4?  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||lowCG||08-29-2001||07:18 PM||||For me it was "gee,do I have to use all that nasty old stuff?"<BR>My rotting exhaust system/manifolds,old sensors,small valve heads,funky old distributor,funky old alternator,and starter wound up under a house foundation in south Detroit somewhere.<BR>I guess I just bought the performance technology in one package('97 DOHC) instead of trying to piece it all together;I added it up against a pushrod rebuild with all the needed repairs/mods,and it just made sense to do it that way.<BR>You don't need to drill for the starter with the 4 cam motor.<BR>There are a few other little things that need to be done with a small grinder,and drill that I can help you with if you decide to go with the newer motor.<BR>I don't believe the aluminum heads will work with the Fiero intake(different ports,and spacing),and have no idea whether or not they'll work on a 3.4.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Dave Gunsul||08-29-2001||09:12 PM||||The alum. heads won't work with the Fiero manifold and accessories. <P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||4
Z||000006||PBJ||08-29-2001||09:20 PM||||The 3.4L with iron heads in the frt wheel drive setup where in mid 90's U van (transport, avp, siluette)<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/forsalestart.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||dennis_6||08-29-2001||09:40 PM||||Thanks for all the info I got alot of thinking to do.  I might look for a 92 Lumina or something with a 3.4 TDC that runs for the engine, but I really hate to cut my decklid supports.||||reg||10
Z||000008||terryk||08-30-2001||01:23 AM||||The 660 in the U-van from 90-94 was a 3.1L not 3.4L. The heads are iron. Great starting point for a 3.1L+ engine.||||reg||1
